  {
    "episode_id": 51279,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51279.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 205,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. And the other thing I called in a little bit, I didn't get to hear the first couple of callers or what the topic was. But so security, I started paying when I was 16. I was forced to, you know, do a W nine and all that. which is cool. I liked working out as always been a good worker. And so I paid in over my career. I don't know what I averaged for a yearly wage. Excuse me. But I paid in like 6.2% Social Security tax. And at the same time, my employer, he paid in 6.4% Social Security tax for total about 12.6. So it's not like free money. It's so security. Over the course of my career, I kind of averaged it out. I didn't make a boatload of money. I had some great years my last 15 years, but so I paid him about all over 300,000. And then the government takes that money and if they want to invest it, if they're smart, they can make money off of it. They can make another 150 grand for maybe a total package of 500,000 that's going to come back to me. in yearly, monthly yearly payments. So so scary is not free. Right. It's a it's a tax. It's like an IRA that the government is the in charge of the IRA. And they could take that IRA money and invest it if they don't choose and make money off of it. And so that's all I got to say about that.",
